ng strong can actually be detrimental  to the patient and the resident yes  that's what the evidence shows  unequivocally a Harvard study found that  residents made almost 36 percent more  serious medical errors when working  four hours or longer they're coming up  against the limits of their of their  capacity to function and all they can  think about is sleep about to start year  to dr. guru therus says extended hours  might have enhanced her first year  training I can read about it I can watch  YouTube I can do anything from home but  it's not gonna be the same as you know  being bedside with a patient so you  haven't heard a lot of commiserating  amongst the residents about how many  hours you have to work and about fatigue  obviously some people get we can get  tired I think that the idea of the  intern is a creature that lives in the  hospital and comes out after 30 hours in  like a dark closet I think that's kind  of archaic now keep up the good work a  key goal of the new rules is to help  doctors become more invested in their  patients by avoiding a shift work  mentality so I go to talk to you in the  green room before you come on and I said  who could possibly think this is a great  idea and you go I actually do and all of  us at the table going what I know it's  complicated issues definitely are  exhausted but I do think it can enhance  learning because you're there to see the  arc of the disease and you do feel more  invested in the patient because it's  like your family you want to show you  everywhere you are you are a higher  earning but what about the patient's  quality well you're training doctors for  the rest of their life though so you  want to make sure they're you know as  well-trained as possible preserving  safety for Thank You Terry okay doctor

